   <front>
     <title>Character Generator Process</title>
     <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="429" stream="IETF"/>
+    <author fullname="Jonathan B. Postel" initials="J." surname="Postel">
+      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">UCLA-NMC</organization>
+      <address/>
+    </author>
     <date year="1972" month="December"/>
     <abstract pn="section-abstract">
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">I hereby propose that there be a standard process implemented on
+whatever hosts desire which generates character data with out any
+regard to input. In some sense the process is the converse of the
+discard process [RFC 348]. Many hosts have an existing terminal
+testing program [e.g. TTYTST] which would suit this function
+adequately.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-2">This Character Generator process would listen for a request for
+connection and execute the Initial Connection Protocol (ICP) as
+specified in NIC 7104 the &quot;Current Network Protocols&quot; notebook.  Upon
+completion of the ICP the Character Generator process would begin to
+send characters into the network as buffer space is made available .</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-3">The Character Generator process is terminated by closing the
+connections.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-4">[This RFC was put into machine readable form for entry into the online RFC archives by BBN Corp. under the direction of Alex McKenzie. 1/97]</t>
     </abstract>
